class:: RhythmTransform
summary:: Per-band modulation magnitude and phase
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Rhythm
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/RhythmPattern, Classes/FourierTempogram, Classes/BarkBands

description::
Measures how quickly each input envelope changes. Each band gets a trailing Hann window and a direct complex Fourier projection at a linear grid of modulation frequencies in Hz. This is an independent MIR2 implementation of ordinary windowed Fourier analysis, extended from one novelty curve to a band-by-modulation matrix. It can distinguish, for example, a slow bass pulse from faster treble activity that a single summed novelty curve conceals.

Control values are averaged over a whole number of control blocks. Held FFT features are interpreted as a continuous, zero-order-held envelope; they are not counted as separate FFT observations. This boxcar averaging offers limited antialiasing: keep envelope bandwidth below the actual feature Nyquist frequency and use a sufficiently short upstream FFT hop. Frequencies above 0.4 times the actual feature rate are rejected. The first matrix arrives after a full history, then outputs hold between analyses. Effective time resolution depends on windowTime, not just the number of modulation bins.

There are at most 1024 output channels, including phase. The product of bands, bins and actual history frames must not exceed 524288; otherwise the server rejects construction. Tables and history are allocated once. No allocation occurs in processing. The closest vanilla tool is FFT applied to an envelope; RhythmTransform provides arbitrary-Hz projections, explicit per-band layout, and timing in seconds without an external control-to-audio conversion.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: bands
Literal Array containing 1–32 control-rate or scalar envelopes. Signed inputs are allowed. Nonfinite samples become zero and samples are bounded to plus/minus 1e12.
argument:: numBins
Literal number of modulation bins per band, 2–128; default 16.
argument:: minHz
Lowest modulation frequency, at least zero; default 0.5 Hz.
argument:: maxHz
Highest modulation frequency, greater than minHz; default 8 Hz. Must be at most 0.4 times both the requested and actual feature rates.
argument:: windowTime
Trailing history in seconds, 0.25–12; default 4. Rounded upward to whole feature frames. Outputs are zero until this history fills.
argument:: hopTime
Matrix update interval in seconds, 0.02–2; default 0.1. Rounded upward to whole feature frames.
argument:: featureRate
Requested envelope sample rate, 20–200 Hz; default 100. Pool size is round(controlRate / featureRate), at least one block. Actual feature rate is controlRate divided by this pool size.
argument:: normalization
Literal symbol: code::\none:: retains coherent-gain-corrected amplitudes in input units; code::\global:: gives the whole magnitude matrix unit L2 norm; code::\band:: normalizes each band separately. Silence remains zero. Default code::\none::.
argument:: removeMean
Literal 1 subtracts the Hann-weighted mean before projection; 0 retains DC. Default 1.
argument:: phase
Literal 1 appends a second matrix of phase values in radians, in [-pi, pi]; 0 gives magnitudes only. Phase uses a cosine convention referred to the newest pooled sample, whose timestamp is the center of its pooling interval. Near-zero coefficients have phase zero. Default 0.

method:: frequencies
Returns the modulation-frequency grid for minHz, maxHz and numBins. Defaults match kr.

returns::
A flat band-major Array: all numBins magnitudes of band zero, then band one, etc. If phase is 1, all phase channels follow all magnitudes. Use code::values.clump(numBins):: to form rows (or first take the magnitude portion when phase is enabled). All parameters except the envelope values are fixed at construction.

examples::
RhythmTransform measures per-band envelope modulation frequencies across an absolute Hz grid, exposing how rhythmic energy fluctuates independently within different spectral registers. In this example, 4 frequency bands are analyzed into 8 modulation bins ($0.5 \dots 8$ Hz): modulation peaks drive a multiband resonant vocoder tracking acoustic drum grooves. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.heatmap:: displays the 32-cell modulation matrix.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 32);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic drum fallback
            var trig = Impulse.kr(2);
            var kick = SinOsc.ar(EnvGen.ar(Env([120, 45], [0.08], \exp), trig)) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.3), trig);
            var snare = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.15), Impulse.kr(2, 0.5));
            (kick + snare) * 0.5
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var band1 = LPF.ar(source, 250);
        var band2 = BPF.ar(source, 600, 0.5);
        var band3 = BPF.ar(source, 2000, 0.5);
        var band4 = HPF.ar(source, 5000);
        var envelopes = [
            Amplitude.kr(band1, 0.005, 0.02),
            Amplitude.kr(band2, 0.005, 0.02),
            Amplitude.kr(band3, 0.005, 0.02),
            Amplitude.kr(band4, 0.005, 0.02)
        ];
        var matrix = RhythmTransform.kr(envelopes, numBins: 8, minHz: 0.5, maxHz: 8.0, windowTime: 2.0, normalization: \band);
        var clampedMatrix = matrix.clump(8);

        // Resonant modulation pulses
        var bassPulse = SinOsc.ar(80, 0, clampedMatrix[0][2].lag(0.05) * 0.2);
        var snarePulse = SinOsc.ar(440, 0, clampedMatrix[2][3].lag(0.05) * 0.12);
        var sound = (source * 0.25 ! 2) + Pan2.ar(bassPulse + snarePulse, 0.0);

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, matrix.clip(0, 1)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.heatmap("Rhythm Transform", ~mir2Bus, 32);
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

